IPC Methods: Pipes, Message Queues, and Signals
Inter-Process Communication (IPC) allows processes to exchange data and coordinate with each other.

1. Pipes
A pipe is a communication channel between processes.

Data written by one process can be read by another.

Usually unidirectional.

Commonly used between related processes, such as a parent and child.

Example: ls | grep ".txt"

Types:

Unnamed pipes – generally used between related processes.

Named pipes (FIFO) – can be used by unrelated processes.

2. Message Queues
A message queue stores messages sent between processes.

Processes can send and receive messages asynchronously.

Messages can often have different types/priorities.

Unlike pipes, communication is message-oriented rather than simply a stream of bytes.

3. Signals
A signal is a small notification sent to a process to indicate that an event has occurred.

It carries very little information.

Used mainly for process control and synchronization.

Examples:

SIGINT – interrupt (Ctrl+C)

SIGTERM – request termination

SIGKILL – force termination

SIGSTOP – stop a process

In short:
Pipes → data streams | Message queues → structured messages | Signals → event notifications
